# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Some tokens contain blacklists that basically control who can transfer the token. If an address is on the certain list, that address can't transfer tokens, they are basically stuck on his address until he gets unblocked.

Some of the most popular tokens like `USDC` and `USDT` use blacklists.

When someone calls `executeWithdrawal` the underlying `asset` tokens are sent to his address or if he is sanctioned by Chainanalysis, the tokens are transferred to an escrow.

The problem occurs, when a lender gets blacklisted and isn't sanctioned by Chainanalysis. The protocol will attempt to transfer the tokens directly to the lender, but since he is blacklisted by the underlying asset token, the tx will revert. Since there is no way to force the tokens to be sent to an escrow, the tokens become stuck inside the market, which can lead to unexpected problems to the borrower, specifically he can be forced into delinquency and his `borrowableAssets` will decrease significantly.

